# No Git LFS any more, deliberately.
#
# Until 2026-09-12 four rules routed *.log, *.pcap, *.bin and the
# ShadowPCDisplay binary through LFS. Every file they covered has moved to
# `shadow2switch-lab/` beside the checkout, so ZERO tracked files match them
# today -- and keeping the filters would force `git lfs` on anyone cloning a
# 974-file source repo for nothing.
#
# That material used to be in the HISTORY as well: the old initial commit
# `1c2d0b1` held tls_plain.log, 75 MB, and with it a live ED25519 SSH private
# key in the clear. This paragraph used to end "to be done ONCE, immediately
# before the repository is ever made public".
#
# DONE, and verified 2026-10-02. The repository IS public now, so a stale
# warning about a live key sitting in public history is itself a problem - it
# would send the next reader hunting for something that is gone. Replaced by
# the check:
#   - `1c2d0b1` is no longer a valid object name, so the rewrite happened;
#   - across ALL 1069 objects in the history, zero file name matches
#     tls_plain / *.pcap / *.log / sslkeys / *.har / refresh_token, and zero
#     blob holds a PEM private key with a body.
# Re-run those two checks before believing this paragraph.

# Encoding: one line ending, whatever the platform of the day.
*.md   text eol=lf
*.c    text eol=lf
*.h    text eol=lf
*.cpp  text eol=lf
*.hpp  text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Patches are applied with `git apply` to trees cloned with LF endings: a
# CRLF checkout (a Windows runner's default) makes every hunk fail. Never
# converted, in either direction.
*.patch -text
# Read by bash or by a toolchain that must see LF on every host.
*.env           text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.ld            text eol=lf
*.in            text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt  text eol=lf
